A single landing page takes 3 to 7 days to build. A standard 3 to 5 page business website takes 1 to 3 weeks, a content-rich 6 to 10 page site takes 2 to 4 weeks, and anything with e-commerce or custom functionality takes 4 to 8 weeks or more. Those numbers are build times once your content is ready, and that qualifier decides almost everything. The websites that land on schedule are the ones where the copy was written and the photos were taken before the first design file was opened. This guide gives you the real timelines from eight live projects, what happens in each stage, and the specific things that turn a two-week build into a two-month one.

## How Long Does It Take to Build a Website?

Here is the full scale, by what you are actually building.

**A single landing page: 3 to 7 days.** One page with a custom design, responsive layout, contact form and basic SEO setup. Suitable for a personal brand, a solo service provider or a product launch.

**A standard business website, 3 to 5 pages: 1 to 3 weeks.** Custom design across several page templates, a CMS so you can edit your own content, full SEO setup and performance work. This is the most common small business project.

**A content-rich site, 6 to 10 pages: 2 to 4 weeks.** Everything above plus a blog, multiple content types, galleries, animations and more extensive schema markup.

**E-commerce or custom functionality: 4 to 8 weeks or more.** Online stores, booking systems, user accounts and payment processing. Testing takes as long as building, because money is involved.

The build itself is the predictable part of that estimate. I can tell you with reasonable confidence how many days the design and development take, because I have done it enough times. What I cannot predict is how long it takes you to send the About page copy. That is not a criticism of clients, it is just where the variance in every website project timeline actually lives. Cost follows the same pattern, and I break the numbers down in my guide to [what a freelance web developer costs](/blog/web-developer-cost).

## What Do Real Website Timelines Look Like?

Ranges are easy to publish and hard to verify, so here are eight projects that are live right now and how long each one took.

- [Comira](/blog/comira), a fitness platform landing page: 3 days
- [Porta Brzi Brod](/blog/porta-brzi-brod), a restaurant site in two languages: 4 days
- [Doorly](/blog/doorly), a real estate landing page: 1 week
- [Victoria](/blog/victoria), a business coaching site: 1 week
- [Casera](/blog/casera), a hotel site built for direct bookings: 2 weeks
- [Advisia](/blog/advisia), a consulting firm website: 2 weeks
- [Vistal](/blog/vistal), an architecture firm redesign: 2 weeks
- [Zvonačka Banja](/blog/zvonacka-banja), a tourism site for a thermal spa: 2 weeks

Two things are worth reading out of that list. First, nothing took months. Second, and more useful to you: the fastest project was not the simplest one. Comira finished in three days because the client had his copy written, knew what he wanted, and answered every question the same day. Porta Brzi Brod shipped in four days with two full language versions, which is more work than several of the two-week projects, because the owner supplied the menu, the photos and the Serbian and English text up front.

Content readiness beats scope every time. A five-page site with finished copy launches faster than a one-page site whose text is still being written.

## What Happens in Each Stage of the Build?

For a standard business website on a 1 to 3 week timeline, the web development process steps break down roughly like this.

**Brief and scope, 1 to 2 days.** Page list, what each page has to do, whether you need a CMS, what content exists and what is missing. This is where realistic dates get set. If content is missing, the honest timeline starts when it arrives, not today.

**Design or wireframe, 2 to 4 days.** Layout for every page template, plus one round of revisions. How long it takes to design a website depends heavily on whether real copy exists, because designing around placeholder text guarantees rework once the real words arrive.

**Build, 3 to 7 days.** Turning the design into a working responsive site: layouts, animations, CMS structure, forms, SEO setup. This stage is the most predictable one in the whole project.

**Review and revisions, 2 to 4 days.** Entirely client-paced. Same-day feedback keeps this at two days. Feedback that arrives next week makes this the longest stage of the project.

**Testing and launch, 1 to 2 days.** Cross-browser and cross-device checks, form testing, accessibility, schema, sitemap and analytics. Performance gets verified here against real thresholds: Google's guidance is that a good [Largest Contentful Paint](https://web.dev/articles/lcp) is 2.5 seconds or less at the 75th percentile of page loads, which is what I aim for before a site goes live rather than after.

Add those up and a standard site is 9 to 19 working days. That is where the 1 to 3 week range comes from.

## Why Do Most Website Projects Take Longer Than Planned?

In order of how often it actually happens.

**Content is not ready.** By a wide margin the biggest cause. The build finishes and the site sits waiting for an About page, three product descriptions and a team photo. Nothing else on this list comes close.

**Feedback loops are slow.** A two-week build with three review rounds absorbs a week of extra calendar time if each round takes three days to come back. It is not the developer waiting that costs you, it is the context switching in between.

**Scope grows mid-build.** A blog added in week two, a booking form in week three. Each addition is reasonable on its own, and each one moves the launch date. Better to launch a focused site now and add phase two later, once the foundation is live.

**Too many decision makers.** Three people with equal authority and different opinions turn a two-day revision round into a two-week negotiation. Name one person who signs off.

**Third-party dependencies.** Domain access nobody can find, a payment provider verification that takes ten days, a booking system whose API documentation is wrong. These are unpredictable, so flag them in week one rather than week three.

## Why Do Agencies Quote 2 to 3 Months for the Same Website?

Search this question and you will find agency articles quoting 8 to 16 weeks next to site builders promising a weekend. Both are being truthful about different things.

Agency timelines are longer for four honest reasons. A formal discovery phase with research and stakeholder interviews can take two weeks before design starts. Approvals route through account managers and several stakeholders, which adds days to every decision. Your project sits in a queue alongside other clients rather than being worked on continuously. And the scope is frequently larger: brand work, copywriting and a marketing campaign delivered alongside the site is genuinely a longer project than a five-page website.

None of that is padding. It is a different service, and for a large multi-stakeholder organisation it is often the right one. But if you are a small business with a defined scope, you are comparing quotes for two different projects. I wrote a full breakdown of how a [freelancer and an agency compare](/blog/freelance-developer-vs-agency) on cost, speed and risk, including the situations where the agency is the correct choice.

The weekend promise from site builders is also true, with an equally important asterisk: it describes filling in a template with your own text, not a custom site with a CMS, SEO setup and performance work.

## What Can You Do to Make Your Website Launch Faster?

Almost all of the leverage sits on your side of the project, which is good news. Use this as a website launch checklist before the build starts.

**Write the copy first.** Even rough final-intent text beats placeholder. This single item is worth more than everything else combined.

**Gather images at full resolution.** Original files, not screenshots and not images pulled off your old site at 400 pixels wide. If you need a photographer, book the shoot before the build begins.

**Name one decision maker.** Collect opinions internally if you like, but one person sends the feedback.

**Book your review slots in advance.** Put two review windows in the calendar when the project starts. It sounds trivial and it routinely saves a week.

**Sort out access early.** Domain registrar login, hosting, analytics, any third-party accounts. Chasing a domain password nobody has is a classic launch-day delay.

**Keep phase two as phase two.** Write down every idea that arrives mid-build and revisit the list after launch. A live site earning its keep beats a perfect site still in review.

If you want to sanity check a developer's estimate before you commit, my guide on [what to look for in a web developer](/blog/what-to-look-for-in-a-web-developer) covers the questions that reveal whether a timeline is realistic or optimistic.

## How Long After Launch Before Your Site Shows Up on Google?

This is usually the real question behind the deadline, and it has a separate answer from the build.

Getting crawled takes days to weeks. Google's own documentation on [asking Google to recrawl your URLs](https://developers.google.com/search/docs/crawling-indexing/ask-google-to-recrawl) states that crawling can take anywhere from a few days to a few weeks. Searching your own business name is the first thing to check, and that generally starts working inside that window.

Ranking for competitive terms is a different track entirely, measured in months, and it depends on content and links rather than on the build. What the build can do is remove every technical obstacle: a sitemap submitted in Search Console on launch day, clean canonical URLs, schema markup, fast pages and no accidental noindex. That is the part I handle at launch. The rest is a content and authority job that starts the day the site goes live.

So if you have a hard date, work backwards from it: the site should be live at least two to four weeks before you need people finding it in search.

## Working to a Deadline?

Realistic planning numbers: 3 to 7 days for a landing page, 1 to 3 weeks for a standard business website, 2 to 4 weeks for something content-heavy, and longer for e-commerce. Have your content ready and those numbers hold. Do not, and no developer alive can hold them for you.

If you have a date in mind, [tell me what you are building and when you need it live](/contact) and I will tell you straight away whether it is realistic, including when it is not. You can also browse [my work and how I approach projects](/) to see what these timelines produce.
